supermox-ve (0.1.24) unstable; urgency=medium

  * supermox-join cluster-init: a new subcommand, and
    src/supermox/corosync_cluster.py behind it. `supermox-join create` now
    writes the cluster's own /etc/corosync/corosync.conf and corosync's
    pre-shared authkey as part of creating the cluster, so pmxcfs and
    corosync describe the cluster from the first boot instead of waiting for
    a step that used to be nobody's job: a node created before this change
    had a membership state file, an overlay subnet and a wg0 tunnel, and no
    corosync nodelist naming its peers, which is what left /etc/pve
    read-only and every join step after it failing. cluster-init is
    idempotent and touches nothing but the nodelist, so it is also the
    repair for a cluster whose corosync step failed halfway, and
    init.d/supermox-wg runs it after regenerating wg0.conf. The config
    create writes lists only this node (4049a76): peers arrive through
    supermox-join core, so a half-joined cluster never carries a nodelist
    entry for a node it has no key for. init.d/supermox-corosync only
    rewrites a config carrying its own header and naming one node; a
    multi-node config belongs to a cluster and is never touched.
    config/shipped-scripts.txt ships the new module, and
    tests/py/test_corosync_cluster.py, tests/py/test_join_cli.py,
    tests/test-init-corosync.sh and tests/test-init-wg.sh cover the create
    path, the repair, the single-node block and the boot script leaving a
    cluster config alone.
  * supermox-join core: makes this node a corosync core member. It asks a
    member's daemon for this cluster's corosync.conf and authkey over the
    new CORE_REQ -> CORE_ACK message pair (src/supermox/joinproto.py,
    answered by src/supermox/joind.py) and hands both to upstream's
    finish_join, which stops pve-cluster, backs up the pmxcfs database,
    starts corosync and pve-cluster, waits for quorum and regenerates the
    node certificate - so nothing in SuperMox duplicates that sequence, and
    nothing is written before a member has actually agreed. The node's
    previous single-node config is moved to corosync.conf.supermox-single
    first, upstream's own rollback: a node that fails halfway through
    becoming a core member must still be able to boot the cluster it had
    before. A node carrying VMs or containers is refused, as upstream does.
  * src/supermox/joind.py listens on the IPv6 address as well as the IPv4
    one, and bin/supermox-join brackets overlay addresses when it builds an
    endpoint (5bceb3a): an overlay address is IPv6, and an unbracketed
    endpoint string lets the parser split the address itself on its last
    colon, so every request to a member reached over the wg0 tunnel went to
    the wrong host. init.d/supermox-joind passes both addresses.
  * supermox-join pending --json and members --json: one JSON array each for
    the Manager (id, name, arch, age_s; name, role, overlay_addr, arch,
    caps, core). core is true exactly when that member appears in
    /etc/pve/corosync.conf's nodelist, which is how the Manager tells a
    corosync member from an overlay member, and an empty members array means
    'this node has no cluster' rather than an error.
  * A join request waiting for approval no longer blocks supermox-join core,
    and joind no longer refuses a CORE_REQ while one is queued: the pending
    request comes from a node that is not a member yet, while core only ever
    asks a node already in the membership list, so the two never interact.
    The guests check is the real precondition. tests/py/test_joind.py and
    tests/py/test_join_cli.py now expect a CORE_ACK and a completed core
    join while a join sits in the queue.
  * Pairs with pve-firewall 6.0.6+supermox3
    (patches/pve-firewall/0004-cluster-traffic.patch): with the host
    firewall on, supermox-joind's TCP 8743, the wg0 tunnel's UDP 51820 and
    corosync knet's UDP port range were all dropped by the default
    policy_in, so a joined node stopped answering CORE_REQ and join traffic
    and the cluster never converged. tests/test-firewall-rules.sh generates
    the rules offline through upstream's own PVE::Firewall module and checks
    the ports and the per-address pairing.

 -- SuperMox <noreply@supermox.me>  Fri, 06 Nov 2026 02:03:46 -0800

supermox-ve (0.1.23) unstable; urgency=medium

  * init.d/pve-cluster: manage pmxcfs by executable instead of by pidfile. pmxcfs
    daemonises itself, and the old stop ran
      start-stop-daemon --stop --pidfile /run/pve-cluster.pid --retry TERM/10/KILL/5
    with no --exec, so whenever that file was missing or stale stop killed nothing
    and returned rc=1 while pmxcfs kept running. PVE::SysvService::stop dies on a
    nonzero rc, so every join step that restarts pve-cluster aborted, and status
    reported "not running" on a node whose /etc/pve was mounted and readable. stop
    now matches --exec /usr/sbin/pmxcfs, and start refuses to launch a second
    instance (pidof check first, and start-stop-daemon's own --start returns 1
    when the executable is already running).
  * start now waits for the pmxcfs FUSE mount of /etc/pve before seeding
    /etc/pve/storage.cfg and writing the pidfile, and unmounts a /etc/pve left
    mounted by a pmxcfs that died - the boot path used to write storage.cfg into
    the empty mountpoint and then mount over it, so the seed was invisible and
    `pct list` reported "cannot access '/etc/pve/.version': Read-Only Filesystem".
  * status returns LSB 0 only when pmxcfs runs AND /etc/pve is mounted, and stop
    unmounts /etc/pve only once no pmxcfs is alive, so a stopped node reports 3
    instead of 0 and a wedged mount is cleared rather than shadowed.
  * tests/test-init-pve-cluster.sh: stubbed pmxcfs, findmnt and start-stop-daemon
    cover stop with a missing and a stale pidfile, stop clearing a dead pmxcfs's
    mount, start not double-starting, start waiting for the mount, status 0/3,
    restart, and that storage.cfg is seeded only inside the mount.

 -- SuperMox <noreply@supermox.me>  Fri, 06 Nov 2026 11:10:42 -0800
supermox-ve (0.1.22) unstable; urgency=medium

  * init.d/supermox-corosync: a new boot script that writes the single node's
    own /etc/corosync/corosync.conf before corosync starts, and rewrites it when
    the hostname or the DHCP address moved. pve-cluster's postinst can no longer
    own this: inside the ISO build chroot `hostname` and `hostname -I` are the
    build machine's, and the PC ISO booted with
      corosync: failed to parse bindnet address 'localhost.localdomain'
    which left /etc/pve read-only and broke every PVE service after it. The
    file is deliberately NOT a conffile of supermox-ve: the corosync package
    already ships /etc/corosync/corosync.conf as its own conffile, and two
    packages claiming one path is a hard install-time file conflict, so
    rewriting it every boot needs no dpkg involvement at all. A config this
    script did not write and that does not carry our own header line is a real
    multi-node cluster config and is never touched.
  * init.d/supermox-hosts now orders supermox-corosync before corosync, and
    init.d/pve-cluster requires supermox-corosync, so pmxcfs only starts once
    that file describes the node that is booting.
  * tests/test-init-corosync.sh: the placeholder is replaced, re-running changes
    nothing, a moved address or a renamed node rewrites the one node block,
    a multi-node config is left alone, no non-loopback address exits 0 without
    blocking the boot, status returns LSB 0/3 as appropriate, stop never deletes
    the file, and the LSB headers keep the order the boot depends on.

 -- SuperMox <noreply@supermox.me>  Fri, 06 Nov 2026 18:04:30 -0800

supermox-ve (0.1.21) unstable; urgency=medium

  * etc/dpkg/dpkg.cfg.d/supermox-no-systemd-units: a new conffile with three dpkg
    path-excludes (/lib/systemd/*, /usr/lib/systemd/*, /etc/systemd/*). Stock Devuan
    packages that ARE installed (ssh, lightdm, avahi, nfs-common, lvm2, lxc,
    wpa_supplicant ...) unpack systemd unit files into those trees; the excludes drop
    them at unpack time, so a node that installs one of those packages later never
    carries systemd unit files. Declared in debian/conffiles for the same reason as
    the apt pins (debhelper only auto-registers conffiles it installs itself). The
    ISO build stages this very file through live-build's includes.chroot_before_packages
    (before any package installs) and adds a chroot hook that removes the unit files
    the debootstrap stage left under those three trees; scripts/build-rootfs.sh already
    passes the same three excludes to mmdebstrap, so the installed node, the ISO and
    the rootfs agree by construction.

supermox-ve (0.1.20) unstable; urgency=medium

  * etc/apt/preferences.d/supermox-origins: hold libpve-network-* at 1.6.7*
    (Pin-Priority: 990). The pinned PXVIRT tree builds libpve-access-control
    9.1.2+supermox1, while the newest libpve-network-perl in the Proxmox
    repository (1.6.8) requires libpve-access-control (>= 9.2.0~), and pve-
    manager pulls libpve-network-perl (>= 1.6.5). A fresh ISO install therefore
    resolved to 1.6.8 and stopped with "Unable to satisfy dependencies", and an
    installed node would have hit the same skew on apt upgrade. The hold keeps
    every libpve-network-* package at the release the pinned tree was cut with,
    so the install resolves and a node can never upgrade into the gap; re-derive
    the version whenever the pinned upstream commit moves. The ISO carries the
    same hold because scripts/build-iso.sh stages this conffile verbatim into
    config/archives/supermox-origins.pref.chroot, so the pin lives in one file
    for the ISO and for the installed node; tests/test-supermox-ve.sh asserts
    the record and tests/test-build-iso.sh asserts the ISO stages the file that
    holds it.

