supermox-ve (0.1.11) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix /etc/init.d/supermox-sync so it can start an instance at all. It exec'd
    "syncthing --port 127.0.0.1:<port> --state <dir>": syncthing has no such
    flags (the daemon subcommand is serve, the state directory is --home and
    the listen address is --gui-address), and it passed start-stop-daemon
    --okstart, which is not an option either (--oknodo is). It now runs
    "syncthing serve --no-browser --no-restart --no-default-folder --home
    /var/lib/supermox-sync/<user> --gui-address http://127.0.0.1:<port>" under
    start-stop-daemon --oknodo. --no-default-folder because serve otherwise
    makes its own "default" share of the user's home on first launch, and
    supermox-sync reconcile already manages that path as the home-<user> folder:
    two Syncthing folders cannot share a path, so reconcile would have failed
    on every fresh instance.
  * The init script's last line is "exit $?" instead of "exit 0", so a user
    whose instance could not start (deleted account, bad port) makes "service
    supermox-sync start" exit 1 rather than lie success; an empty user list is
    still success. Its empty-list message now says whether the file is missing
    or simply lists no users.
  * Ship debian/postinst creating /etc/supermox/sync-users when absent, with a
    comment giving the format (one user per line, file order fixes the port).  Nothing
    else created it, and both the init script and /usr/bin/supermox-sync read
    it and fail without it. It never overwrites an existing file, and it does
    not touch rc links or start services: debhelper's own snippet does that.

